      ⍝ regression (Blake McBride, Bugs31 #11): Smith's algorithm (Bugs30
      ⍝ #6) divides by the larger-magnitude component first to keep every
      ⍝ intermediate bounded -- but "bounded" still meant as large as
      ⍝ fabs(real)+fabs(imag), which itself overflows once both
      ⍝ magnitudes approach DBL_MAX/2, even though the true reciprocal is
      ⍝ a perfectly representable (possibly subnormal) finite value.
      ⍝ Fixed by prescaling both components by their common larger
      ⍝ magnitude before applying Smith's algorithm.
